From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Robert McFadden <robert.mcfadden@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 12:40:52 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] Red Dead Redemption's distant blocks are gone: a draw that
 samples the depth attachment its render pass already wrote ends the pass
 first, so it reads what was written

Red Dead Redemption's cloud fog (rdr2_clouds_fog) fades the clouds against the scene's depth, sampling the depth attachment of the colour pass it draws in, after the tree cards, low terrain and props of that pass wrote depth (no game barrier between them). On Apple's GPUs an attachment sampled within the pass that writes it reads whole compressed blocks of what the tiles stored so far: the fog took 8x8 blocks above the trees for geometry, faded the clouds out there and left bare sky, different blocks every frame (a flicker on distant hills and trees in cutscenes and play).

fb_prepare already ended the pass for the other order (a depth or stencil write after a draw that read the attachment) and snapshots colour attachments a pass wrote; this order was not covered. Now a draw that samples the depth attachment, bound or named in a uniform buffer, after a draw of its pass wrote depth or stencil ends the pass before it (not when the draw itself writes depth). On Red Dead Redemption's Armadillo route about one pass end a frame: GPU p50 14.3 -> 14.7 ms, frame time unchanged. DM_NVN_FB_DEPTH=0 turns it off.

Upstream-Status: forwarded seedds/nxnative#1866
Reason: Red Dead Redemption's sky showed flickering blocks over distant hills: a draw sampling the depth its pass had written read partly stored tiles
Issue: LNPANDY/NX2Apple#999

---
 dm/gfx/nvn/nvn_mtl.m        | 19 +++++++++++++++----
 docs/env_switches.tsv       |  1 +
 docs/reference/rendering.md |  8 ++++++++
 3 files changed, 24 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/dm/gfx/nvn/nvn_mtl.m b/dm/gfx/nvn/nvn_mtl.m
